Date: Sun, 12 Dec 1993 22:02:35 -0500 (EST)
From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: Human Language (was The Importance of Reading)

James Baker writes:

>I agree that writing is one of the major inventions of all time and in fact
>very cool but what about _talking_?  My read of pre-history says that for
>most of our 6 million year humanoid existence we didn't have verbal
>communications and lived pretty much like other mammals. 

This is a problem of definition.  Many scholars believe that it *is* the 
ability to communicate verbally that defines the term "human".  It has even 
been proposed that spoken communication arose as a result of the need to use 
the hands for skilled activities other than communication (e.g., tool-making 
and -using).  

>Then around say, 200,000 years ago human evolution took a turn quite
>different from the evolution of say dogs and sheep; (ie. agriculture, fire,
>cities, trade....) My guess is that the invention of spoken language was
>the reason.

Human language has been around far, far longer than agriculture, cities, and 
trade. (Fire I'm not so sure about.)  There are still some nomadic 
hunter-gatherer societies, and they do have their own languages.  Though these 
people may not have a tradition of written literature, their languages are 
syntactically, phonologically, morphologically, and semantically as complex as 
English, French, Japanese, et al.  (If you don't believe this, try learning an 
Austronesian or Niger-Congo language.)

>As an interesting footnote our vocal tracts, not our intelligence may be
>the reason that we're homo-sapiens, not Neanderthals.

Neanderthal vocal tracts were probably capable of producing a large sub-set of 
the sounds that we can produce now.  (The source of this information is an 
article by Philip Lieberman in an early issue of _Linguistic Inquiry_; I can 
dig up the reference if anyone really wants it.)  Most likely, our vocal tracts 
and intelligence co-evolved in a mutually reinforcing way.

In general, I think it's important to distinguish between an *invention* like 
writing, and a biological trait, like language.

-- Simon!



