From: Jeff Fabijanic <jeff@PROFESSOR-ED.ELF.COM>
Subject: The Importance of Reading

Ray writes:
>   Well, this is not my area of study but it seems to me that spoken language
>has more to do with our biology and less to do with memes/culture.

I meant it the other way around. When I said "I think spoken language is
our "first and greatest" [invention] (and the concept of "culture" that
that implies)" I was speaking of the phenomena of human culture as emerging
*from* our ability to speak. Culture is the integrated pattern of human
knowledge, belief, and behavior that depends upon man's capacity for learning
and transmitting knowledge to succeeding generations. It's something we've
had as a species for hundreds of thousands of years. 


>I don't
>mean the language itself, but the ability to speak and the way we learn to
>speak. Personally, I think spoken language is partly responsible for our
>sentience. 


I agree.

>I would bet that if two babies were plopped down on an island,
>they would somehow learn to communicate as adults and use tools, but would 

>most likely not discover writing. (e.g. speaking and tool use are partly in 

>the genes, writing is not, or atleast that's my theory)

Hmm, perhaps, but then again, perhaps not. I don't think that they would
be any more likely to figure out fire, or the wheel. I *do* think that
they would figure out containers and levers. But this is all supposition,
with no way I can think of to test it.

>   Reading and writing were what made technology really take off because for
>the first time you could communicate knowledge into the future in a precise
>way instead of via culture. Writing allowed for the printing press which
>obsoleted any other form of communication.

You're playing into my point. I agree with you that written language
appears to have been an incredible catalyst for human cultural development
(btw, the definition of culture includes *all* methods of info transmittal
between people). But it seems to me that we shouldn't assume that just
because a technology has been successful, that a *more* successful technology
won't supplant it. In fact, it happens all the time. The invention of bronze
(around 4000 bc) made for great tools and spurred some spectacular changes in
human civilization. But as great as bronze was for many things, iron (1000 bc)
was even better. Yes, we still use bronze, but we use magnitudes more iron
(steel). Why assume that written phonetic language won't be superseded as well?
Next year? No. But maybe next century, and since I plan on being around much
longer than that, I think it's foolish to get too attached to any one
technology as a way of getting things done.

