Great post Simon! (Hey that's pretty cool - any simple sentence that ends
with your name ends up looking like the writer intended a particularly
forceful statement, whether he did or not. I did, of course ;)

I've seen people make this distinction before, but I have to wonder, is it
reasonable to make the assumption that we *aren't* biologically disposed to
reading and writing? If R&W are such a great advantages, then it seems as
though any humans with a genetic prediliction to readily understand abstract  
visual symbol systems (ie, most modern written languages) would have a leg up
in suceeding in their culture and passing the advantage onto their children.
If good readers and writers made more than their illiterate counterparts, they
would generally be able to afford more food, better health care, superior
shelter and clothing, etc. After a few thousand years of this, might we not
expect there to exist a population shift towards any genomes that potentiated
that ability?

Certain types of dyslexia are said to run in families. Could it be that what
we are seeing there is not a genetic defect, but an atavism?

just a thought,
