Reilly Jones <70544.1227@CompuServe.COM>  sez-

> 	If anyone else has read it, I would like to share my concern that Nozick
> has included what he calls Symbolic Utility along with Consequential Utility and
> Evidential Utility into Decision Value calculations.  To me, this is a
> dangerous, seductive road to travel down. 

People do seem to place symbolic values on things.  We like things
pretty and neat, and this seems almost built into us.  This I wouldn't call
correct but it does seem to be a handy heuristic.

Also, there should be a "simplicity value," having to do with the computational
and mistake-making costs of complicated versus simple situations.  In many 
cases this can only be judged subjectively, and then it's hard to distinguish 
from an irrational preference.

> It awkwardly places value and fact
> together into rational decision making.
...
> 	Morality and rationality cannot be neatly tied together in an equation.
> They are far more messy.  You may be able to rationally arrive at a moral goal,
> but you are left hanging onto nothing when it comes to declaring the moral goal
> rational by itself.  Likewise, you may be able to morally arrive at a rational
> goal, but you are left hanging onto nothing when it comes to declaring the
> rational goal moral by itself.

True, but my tendency is to treat my values as aspects of me as I factually
exist right now.  This turns moral thinking into negotiating with myself.

-fnerd
