Subject: Baby Killing Assault Rifles - Glock 17

> 
> I would like to add something here, with respect to the Glocks.
> They do not have a safety mechanism. (The long trigger pull *is* the
> mechanism.)  IF YOU HAVE CHILDREN AT HOME, PLEASE STORE IN A LOCKED CASE.
> Sorry, didn't mean to yell, I just want to stress the extra caution
> that one must take with the Glocks.
> 
> Amara Graps

Glocks are actually ideal for households with children. Simply explain
to the children that the gun will fire if the trigger is pulled. After
that has been done, anything after that may be thought of as evolution
in action.

(Of course, since the kid is more likely to shoot other kids than
itself, maybe leaving Glocks laying around is not such a great idea.
Of course, a revolver is fired the same way, by each pull of the
trigger.)

On a serious note, safety devices are best thought of as way to keep
the weapon from discharging when a (light) trigger is somehow snagged,
or otherwise moved, not to stop children or foolish adults from
getting it to fire. Thus, the Glock meets the definition of not being
fired except by deliberate pulling of the trigger (especially as the
trigger also has a small safety enhancement, which I won't go into
here).

The best safety method with children around is to:

a. Teach them that guns are _not_ toys and are not to be played with.
Apparently children as young as 4 can get this message.

b. Attach a rubber-band-like "Gun Loaded" strap which can be easily
removed by adults but not by children...this also reminds everyone the
gun is "ready to go" (some folks like to dry fire their guns indoors,
for practice, and they might "forget" that they loaded the gun--cf.
section above about evolution in action).

(The effects of these bands or gun locks on quickly getting a weapon
into play need to be taken into account. Practice removing the bands
or locks, and don't use them for coat- or purse-carried, guns of
course.)

c. Lock them up. The guns, too.


I'd place my faith on a. That is, "teach your children." Relying on
"security through obscurity," that is, hiding the guns or making them
inoperable, simply makes the guns even more attractive and mysterious to
children. And they might find them, defeat the safety features, or
encounter them at friend's houses.

Better to give them memes that will help them survive.

